                <pb facs="0647" n="615" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v15/pg615"/>

                <head>291. Editorial Note<anchor n="1" xml:id="d291-f1"/></head>
                <p>In early January 1958, Ambassador James M.
                        Langley entered into high-level discussions with Pakistani
                    leaders with regard to the proposed establishment in Peshawar of a [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">less than 1 line of text not declassified</hi>]
                    communications facility to be operated by U.S. officials. The establishment of
                    the facility had been requested by the Department of Defense and concurred in by
                    the Department of State. After lengthy negotiations, Pakistan and the United
                    States reached an agreement for the establishment of the communications unit on
                    July 18, 1959; for text of the agreement, which was signed in Karachi by
                    Ambassador Langley and Foreign Minister
                        Manzur Qadir, see 10 UST 1366.
                    Extensive documentation concerning the negotiation of the agreement, operation
                    of the facility, and related matters is in Department of State, Central Files
                    711.56390D for 1958 and 1959 and 711.551 and 790.56311 for 1960.</p>
            </div>
